  "kind": "parity_proof",
  "title": "The square of an odd integer",
  "prompt": "Prove: if $n$ is an odd integer, then $n^2$ is odd.",
  "answer_format": "A structured proof: let, case, expr and witness steps (notes optional). No final answer box.",
  "difficulty": 1,
  "tags": [
    "direct proof",
    "parity"
  ],
  "data": {
    "variables": [
      "n"
    ],
    "hypotheses": [
      {
        "var": "n",
        "is": "odd"
      }
    ],
    "target": "n^2",
    "goal": "odd"
  },
  "mistakes": [],
  "hints": [
    "Start from the definition: an odd integer is 2·(some integer) + 1. Write n that way with a new letter.",
    "Expand n², then group it as 2·(something) + 1. The 'something' is your witness."
  ],
  "solution": [
    "Since $n$ is odd, $n = 2k + 1$ for some integer $k$.",
    "Then $n^2 = (2k+1)^2 = 4k^2 + 4k + 1 = 2(2k^2 + 2k) + 1$.",
    "$2k^2 + 2k$ is an integer (integers are closed under $+$ and $\\times$), so $n^2$ is odd. $\\blacksquare$"
  ],
  "reference": {
    "book": "sundstrom-mr3",
    "section": "1.2 Constructing Direct Proofs",
    "note": "Original problem; see the definitions of even and odd integers."
  },
  "review": {
    "status": "retired",
    "notes": "Invented problem, retired 2026-11-06: MATH 123 proofs now map 1:1 to Book of Proof exercises."
  },
